# Research: REAPER plugin architecture for the Speed Editor

Scope paragraph.

## TL;DR
Numbered list of the key points:
1. The device is a USB HID "keyboard" with a mandatory mutual-authentication handshake. The protocol is public (bmd.py), but there is no OS driver — our plugin (or a companion daemon) has to open the device, authenticate, and poll reports on a worker thread.
2. REAPER plugins don't need anything special: entry point + GetFunc/Register. Everything is registered through rec->Register.
3. Key SDK hooks: hookcommand2 (intercept any action, mouse wheel values pass through), timer (periodic), custom_action, reaper_csurf_reg_t (full control-surface model). Host API CSurf_ScrubAmt moves the cursor in scrub units.
4. Two viable architectures: (A) minimal — translate the wheel into REAPER's built-in scrub/jog action (CC relative) and let REAPER handle the transport semantics; (B) full — register a control surface (IReaperControlSurface) or use timer + hookcommand2, and own the mode state ourselves. The plan is to start with A and evolve toward B.
5. The jog-mode buttons (SHTL/JOG/SCRL) set the device's jog mode via output report 0x03 — the plugin must also write back to the device (LEDs, jog mode) to stay in sync.
6. The hardware/HID dev environment is already in docs/reaper-dev-env.md §7/§8.1 (hidapi + libusb + udev in the devShell; worker-thread polling pattern).

## 1. The device: Blackmagic Speed Editor as a USB HID peripheral
- What it is: USB HID composite device (keyboard + jog + LED). VID 0x1edb / PID 0xda0e. No MIDI.
- Mandatory mutual auth: before the device emits reports, both sides must perform a SET_FEATURE/GET_FEATURE exchange with report ID 6. Public algorithm (bmd_kbd_auth: lookup table + rotation).
- Input reports table: 0x03 jog (mode byte + signed LE32), 0x04 keys (6×LE16, full held set, no auto-repeat), 0x07 battery.
- Output reports: 0x02 main LED (LE32 bit field), 0x03 jog mode (mode, 0, 0xFF), 0x04 jog-mode button LED.
- Jog modes: 0 relative, 1 absolute continuous (±4096 ≈ half a turn, baseline is the value when the mode was set), 2 relative, 3 absolute deadzone.
- Key map: 44 keys. Includes STOP_PLAY 0x3c, JOG 0x1d, CAM1–9, IN/OUT. (The CAM keys are Resolve-specific — on the DAW side we'll remap them.)
- Caveat: protocol is from bmd.py (software reverse engineering, 2011–); we haven't yet verified it against the real hardware. The headless CLI reader is planned to confirm it first.

## 2. Prior art
Table with URLs:
- smunaut/blackmagic-misc bmd.py — Python + python-hid, Apache-2.0. The reference implementation of the Speed Editor HID protocol (auth, reports, jog modes).
- JamesBalazs/speed-editor-client — Go + go-hid: key tables (keys/keys.go), jog modes, LED control. examples/volume_wheel/main.go shows the "wheel → volume" pattern (cross-platform).
- reaper_csurf (in reaper-sdk) — the official control-surface plugin pattern (IReaperControlSurface).
- SWS — the orthodox SDK reference (dev-env doc §5).
- REAPER built-in: "Transport: Scrubbing fine control (MIDI CC relative Only)" — when stopped = scrub, when playing = jog. Tunable in Preferences → Audio → Playback (reaper.blog 2017/12).
- Community "unlock" apps — they make the Speed Editor a plain keyboard (no-plugin option). Coarse: no mode awareness, no LED feedback.
- Sibling project ~/projects/speed-audacity — same hardware, Audacity side. That conclusion: Audacity 4.0.0's plugin format doesn't fit transport control → REAPER plugin is the chosen path. USB/HID approach is shareable.
- Cockos forum t=250358 — a thread about exactly this device. Blocked by bot so we skip.

## 3. How REAPER extensions work (SDK surface we'll use)
- Entry point ReaperPluginEntry(HINSTANCE, reaper_plugin_info_t*). GetFunc for host APIs. Register for everything. No legacy on_main.
- Registration types (with verified line numbers):
  - hookcommand2 (reaper_plugin.h:213–221) — bool onAction(KbdSectionInfo*, int command, int val, int val2, int relmode, HWND). Intercepts every action. val/val2 carry MIDI/OSC/mouse-wheel values. relmode is 0/1/2/3.
  - hookpostcommand2 (:223) — after the action has run. 7 args with ReaProject*.
  - timer (:247–249) — periodic void (*timer_function)().
  - custom_action + action_help (REAPER_custom_action_register_t, ~:1107) — register our own actions.
  - reaper_csurf_reg_t (:1535–1544) — register "csurf". create() → IReaperControlSurface.
  - Host API CSurf_ScrubAmt(double amt) (reaper_plugin_functions.h ~:880) — scrub the cursor by an amount (double is in "scrub units").
- Note: cross-reference dev-env doc §1–§2 for the anatomy of the build/install.

## 4. Architecture: Speed Editor → REAPER transport
- Layered, high-level (no code):
  - L0 Device layer (worker thread): open hidrawN (or hidapi), run the auth handshake, read input reports (0x03/0x04/0x07) on the loop. Write output reports (LED, jog mode). Queue state changes. Never blocks REAPER's UI thread.
  - L1 Device state: current jog value/delta, mode byte, held key set, battery.
  - L2 Mode interpretation: SHTL/JOG/SCRL buttons select the transport mode (shuttle speed vs jog step vs scroll/scrub). Maps to a device jog-mode output + our internal transport intent.
  - L3 REAPER integration (pick one, start minimal):
    - Option A (minimal, first milestone): on jog delta, trigger the built-in scrub/jog action (CC-relative). REAPER itself handles stopped=scrub vs playing=jog. Buttons → REAPER actions (play/stop, etc.) via _trigger_command. No custom action needed.
    - Option B (control surface): register csurf (reaper_csurf_reg_t) and implement IReaperControlSurface: REAPER asks us for transport state and calls our jog/shuttle/scrub methods — bidirectional sync, LED feedback from transport state, proper jog/scrub semantics via CSurf_ScrubAmt / transport APIs.
    - Option C (hook-based fallback): hookcommand2 to intercept the mouse wheel / MIDI CC that a companion maps to, plus timer for periodic UI sync. (The main reason to have hookcommand2: we own the mapping and can intercept anything.)
  - Data flow: worker thread → atomic state / lock-protected queue → REAPER-side consumption (timer or csurf callback) → transport action → optional write-back (LED via output reports).
- Why worker-thread polling: HID reports are event-driven. The plugin has no "input callback" from REAPER. The timer is coarse. Polling at ~1–4 kHz is cheap.

## 5. Build plan / next steps
1. Headless CLI HID reader (C/C++ using hidapi in the existing devShell) — verify the auth + reports against real hardware (dev-env doc §7).
2. Minimal plugin skeleton: entry point + custom_action "speed editor: jog cursor" + a timer that reads the wheel and triggers the built-in scrub action (Option A).
3. Mode buttons → shuttle/jog/scroll state + jog-mode output report + LEDs.
4. Evaluate moving to Option B (csurf) once A is stable.
5. Windows parity later (hid.dll). Cross-reference dev-env doc §7.

